jitCompileOnceT -- SEQ 385 R1 (Tony, 2026-10-19): COMPILE ONCE. A compiled action is reused, never rebuilt, so a
later testing(), jitRefire or forced compile answers from the cache. Born red on trunk.

The jitted half runs first and the interpreted twins last (bear-trap #25). coF is self-calling, so testing(coDrive1)
builds it as its own function, jit_coF, beside jit_coDrive1. Then:
  CO-2  testing(coDrive2) needs coF's own function again. Trunk builds jit_coF a second time and the JIT refuses the
        module ("duplicate definition of symbol"), so nothing runs and co2 stays 999. Cured: coDrive2's module calls
        the jit_coF already in the JIT.
  CO-3  testing(coDrive1) a second time. Trunk: the first parse consumed isCoded, so testing() routes to jitRunIfTest
        and coDrive1 never runs (co1 stays 999). Cured: the cached function answers.
  CO-4  jitRefire fires the last compiled function.
The pop.sh rows also read the compile count and the REUSED lines: two compiles, never three, and the IR of the second
module DECLARES jit_coF instead of defining it.
Intended values are the interpreted twins: coF(4) = 10, coF(3) = 6.
